WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.680
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.530
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:12.440
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.310
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.040
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.000
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.950
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:23.770
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.580
we are going to be talking

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.299
about AI and learning English.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.710
Now, we’ve talked about this before,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.500
back in 2022, but that seems like

00:00:34.519 --> 00:00:37.516
centuries ago in the era of AI.

00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:40.614
So in today’s episode we are going

00:00:40.614 --> 00:00:43.150
to talk about what has changed, what

00:00:43.169 --> 00:00:47.510
hasn’t changed, and how AI can and can’t

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:51.118
help you improve your English in 2026.

00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:53.503
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:53.523 --> 00:00:56.192
minute and get right into it.

00:00:57.793 --> 00:00:59.003
I want you to cast your

00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:01.740
mind back to late 2022.

00:01:02.403 --> 00:01:04.863
The world was watching anxiously

00:01:05.093 --> 00:01:07.223
as the Russian invasion of Ukraine

00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:09.903
looked to be ratcheting up a notch.

00:01:10.293 --> 00:01:12.393
Inflation looked like it might

00:01:12.393 --> 00:01:14.033
be spiralling out of control.

00:01:14.553 --> 00:01:18.423
In the UK, a tabloid newspaper set up a

00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:21.953
livestream of an iceberg lettuce and a

00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:24.943
picture of the then Prime Minister, Liz

00:01:24.993 --> 00:01:28.253
Truss, asking its viewers which would

00:01:28.253 --> 00:01:30.923
last longer, the lettuce or the PM.

00:01:31.653 --> 00:01:33.693
By the way, the lettuce won.

00:01:34.453 --> 00:01:37.783
And then in late November, quietly

00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:40.819
at first, a company called OpenAI

00:01:41.073 --> 00:01:44.883
launched a chatbot called ChatGPT.

00:01:45.873 --> 00:01:47.923
Within five days, a

00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:49.723
million people had used it.

00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:51.943
I was one of them, and

00:01:51.973 --> 00:01:53.633
perhaps you were too.

00:01:54.523 --> 00:01:56.353
It seemed like magic.

00:01:56.573 --> 00:01:58.553
You could type in a question,

00:01:58.813 --> 00:02:00.283
any question, and you

00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:01.473
would get an answer back.

00:02:02.093 --> 00:02:03.213
Help me write this email.

00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:05.413
What’s the capital of Uzbekistan?

00:02:05.863 --> 00:02:07.703
Why is grass green?

00:02:08.693 --> 00:02:11.933
Now, the answers weren't always right.

00:02:12.163 --> 00:02:14.083
Sometimes they were plain wrong.

00:02:14.513 --> 00:02:18.233
But it was a window into the future, a

00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:21.323
future in which knowledge, intelligence,

00:02:21.543 --> 00:02:24.693
was available to anyone, anywhere, in

00:02:24.693 --> 00:02:27.173
a way that it never had been before.

00:02:27.863 --> 00:02:30.043
And it didn’t take long for people

00:02:30.043 --> 00:02:32.293
to start thinking about how this

00:02:32.323 --> 00:02:34.713
new technology could be applied

00:02:35.013 --> 00:02:37.293
to the world of language learning.

00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:40.223
Indeed, just a couple of weeks

00:02:40.463 --> 00:02:43.373
after the release of ChatGPT we

00:02:43.373 --> 00:02:45.693
made an episode on exactly this,

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.333
imaginatively called “Artificial

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:50.043
Intelligence & Language Learning”.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:54.043
That was three and a half years ago, and

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:57.093
things have moved on a lot since then.

00:02:57.573 --> 00:03:00.693
The technology has improved significantly,

00:03:01.073 --> 00:03:02.873
there are many things that are possible

00:03:02.873 --> 00:03:06.153
now that weren’t even a few months ago.

00:03:06.703 --> 00:03:10.193
But with these improvements come pitfalls,

00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:12.983
traps I have seen plenty of people fall

00:03:13.013 --> 00:03:15.733
into, so we will talk about those too.

00:03:16.423 --> 00:03:18.693
Now, a quick administrative note

00:03:18.803 --> 00:03:20.893
before we get too far into it.

00:03:21.373 --> 00:03:22.773
I’m not going to talk too much

00:03:22.773 --> 00:03:26.603
about specific AI tools, ChatGPT vs.

00:03:26.603 --> 00:03:30.033
Gemini or Claude, or any of the speaking

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.703
apps, because they all do pretty similar

00:03:32.713 --> 00:03:36.563
things, and the best one right now might

00:03:36.563 --> 00:03:39.033
not be the best one by the time you listen

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:42.493
to this, so what I’m about to say should

00:03:42.493 --> 00:03:45.333
apply to whatever AI tool you are using.

00:03:46.103 --> 00:03:48.433
I’m also going to be talking relatively

00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:51.123
generally, rather than going into

00:03:51.403 --> 00:03:53.893
specific prompts and exercises.

00:03:54.253 --> 00:03:56.563
If you’d like specific tips and

00:03:56.563 --> 00:03:58.433
tricks, then there are a bunch

00:03:58.683 --> 00:04:01.593
of tutorials over on our YouTube

00:04:01.593 --> 00:04:03.003
channel - I’ll put a link to that

00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:05.383
in the description for this episode.

00:04:05.853 --> 00:04:08.743
OK, with those disclaimers out

00:04:08.743 --> 00:04:11.723
of the way, let’s start by going

00:04:11.723 --> 00:04:14.193
through it semi-systematically.

00:04:15.583 --> 00:04:17.443
The way that many people think

00:04:17.453 --> 00:04:20.093
about language competency is

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:23.783
through the four pillars of reading,

00:04:24.043 --> 00:04:26.913
writing, listening and speaking.

00:04:27.653 --> 00:04:32.683
This is a helpful framing, but I

00:04:32.703 --> 00:04:35.163
think it’s even more helpful to

00:04:35.163 --> 00:04:38.933
split it into just two: input and

00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:41.533
output, with reading and listening

00:04:41.803 --> 00:04:44.353
in the input category, and writing

00:04:44.353 --> 00:04:46.973
and speaking in the output category.

00:04:47.543 --> 00:04:49.993
I think this is a useful framework

00:04:50.343 --> 00:04:53.083
for thinking about where AI can

00:04:53.083 --> 00:04:55.463
fit in, especially as almost

00:04:55.513 --> 00:04:58.723
everyone finds output harder than

00:04:58.773 --> 00:05:01.513
input; it’s easier to understand

00:05:01.523 --> 00:05:03.813
English than it is to produce it.

00:05:04.373 --> 00:05:06.623
So, let’s start with input:

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:08.763
reading first, and then listening.

00:05:09.893 --> 00:05:14.483
Back in 2022, AI was already

00:05:14.553 --> 00:05:17.453
quite good at dealing with text.

00:05:17.693 --> 00:05:19.723
You could paste in an article

00:05:20.126 --> 00:05:21.163
and ask for a summary.

00:05:21.393 --> 00:05:22.323
You could ask it to

00:05:22.323 --> 00:05:24.213
explain a difficult word.

00:05:24.453 --> 00:05:26.793
You could even ask it to simplify

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:29.983
something into B1-level English.

00:05:30.473 --> 00:05:33.263
Super helpful, and that hasn’t changed.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:37.363
What has changed is the scale,

00:05:37.693 --> 00:05:40.303
the speed, and the integration.

00:05:40.833 --> 00:05:44.333
In 2026, almost every website,

00:05:44.463 --> 00:05:46.163
every app, every browser,

00:05:46.443 --> 00:05:49.823
has some form of AI built in.

00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.053
You can highlight a paragraph

00:05:52.053 --> 00:05:53.673
and get an explanation instantly.

00:05:53.973 --> 00:05:55.833
You can generate a

00:05:55.843 --> 00:05:57.813
vocabulary list in seconds.

00:05:57.863 --> 00:06:00.903
You can ask follow-up questions and have a

00:06:00.953 --> 00:06:03.083
conversation about what you’ve just read.

00:06:03.943 --> 00:06:05.703
From a purely technical

00:06:05.723 --> 00:06:08.223
perspective, this is extraordinary.

00:06:08.743 --> 00:06:12.416
But here is the first important point:

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:16.050
AI makes it very easy to consume

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.930
content without it actually sticking,

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.600
without you actually learning from it.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:23.630
You might understand something

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:26.460
in the moment, perhaps you feel

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.090
like you've learned it, but then,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.790
surprise surprise, you forget it.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:31.560
It's gone.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.740
So if you're using AI to help you read

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.108
and understand English, make sure you're

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.980
doing something with what you are taught.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.530
That might be writing the phrase down,

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.890
using it in a sentence, making sure

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:50.220
you come back to it a few days later.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.410
Doing everything you can to use it

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.900
actively, the same as in the pre-AI era.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.420
And in fact, there’s one more thing,

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.690
something you should do before you even

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.420
think about using AI to explain something:

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.080
force yourself to guess first, to really

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.230
think about what the answer could be.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:15.790
If you come across a difficult

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.410
expression, try to work out the meaning

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.830
from the context before asking AI.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.380
If it’s a tricky grammar point, try to

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.160
really THINK about what the rule is,

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.390
then ask AI to check if you're right.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.710
This transforms AI from an

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:37.210
answer machine into a tutor.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.400
You're thinking actively, not just

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.030
consuming passively, so you are much

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:45.790
more likely to retain the information,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.340
you’re much more likely to actually learn.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:52.020
It’s a similar story with listening,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.520
although there are some additional

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:57.140
complications and opportunities.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.110
The main thing to say is that text

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.210
to speech technology, which is

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:05.550
the technology that takes text and

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.520
converts it to spoken language, this

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:12.550
has improved dramatically since 2022.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:17.070
Back then, AI voices sounded robotic.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:17.920
Obvious.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.320
It was quite painful to listen to.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:24.440
Today, synthetic speech is almost

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:28.110
indistinguishable from a real human voice.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:30.280
You can slow it down naturally.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.360
You can change accents.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:35.700
You can even interrupt it mid-sentence

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.730
and ask it for clarification.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:41.580
This can be incredibly useful,

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:44.080
especially for people at lower levels,

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:47.460
let’s say anyone up to B1 or so.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:51.100
Most of the AI tools, from

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.130
ChatGPT to Gemini, they have a

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.270
voice mode, where you can have a

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.670
conversation about whatever you want.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:00.840
We’ll talk about this more in a

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.490
few minutes, when we talk about

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.670
speaking, but for the purposes of

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.780
listening, these models have come

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:12.785
on leaps and bounds since 2022.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.870
The accents are better, it sounds more

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:19.030
natural, and most importantly, it gives

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:23.010
you 24/7 access to an endlessly patient

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.980
and friendly source of English input.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.900
But if you are listening to

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.510
this show, AI-generated English

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:34.210
might be a little frustrating.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:36.250
You are at a level where you can

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.970
understand a lot of native-level

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:42.444
content, and sometimes AI-generated

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.450
content is painfully obvious.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.100
If you’ve ever been confronted with

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.460
these AI-generated podcasts on YouTube,

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:52.130
you’ll know exactly what I mean.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.450
And here’s the danger.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.700
If you spend too much time listening to

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.280
simplified AI-generated English, your

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:04.380
brain will adapt to simplified English.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:06.860
Or English that sounds like

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.190
someone is reading out what is

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:10.790
meant to be a written article.

00:10:11.594 --> 00:10:14.910
AI generated audio will undoubtedly

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.660
improve in the future, but at the moment

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:21.230
it’s great for single words or single

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.170
sentences, but anything longer than that

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:27.230
is usually pretty easy to recognise.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.100
It’s not the English that you’ll find in

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:32.620
the real world, English that’s full of

00:10:32.934 --> 00:10:35.780
ums and ahs, people changing what they

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.000
want to say mid-sentence, mumbling, being

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:43.090
interrupted, real, raw, native English.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:46.360
So here’s how I think about it.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:49.860
The best way to improve your listening

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:54.030
skills remains to listen to real, human,

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.290
raw English, whether that’s podcasts,

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.650
YouTube, audiobooks, TV, or whatever else.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:05.480
But to use AI as a way to scaffold, to

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.440
build on this, and support your listening.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.030
Let me give you a concrete example

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.810
of how this can work with podcasts,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.590
because I think this is one of the

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.620
most powerful uses of AI, and clearly

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.270
it’s relevant to you, because you

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:22.920
are listening to one right now.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:27.410
So, first, listen to the episode normally.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.390
Don't look at the transcript yet.

00:11:29.665 --> 00:11:32.285
Then, before looking at the

00:11:32.295 --> 00:11:35.325
transcript, open up ChatGPT and

00:11:35.335 --> 00:11:37.495
try to summarise what you heard.

00:11:38.415 --> 00:11:42.685
THEN paste in the full transcript and

00:11:42.745 --> 00:11:46.285
ask it to find 10 natural expressions

00:11:46.445 --> 00:11:48.815
that native speakers use; not just

00:11:48.825 --> 00:11:51.525
vocabulary words, but actual phrases.

00:11:52.195 --> 00:11:54.935
For each expression, get AI to

00:11:54.935 --> 00:11:57.785
explain what it means and show you

00:11:57.785 --> 00:12:00.385
how to use it in a different context.

00:12:01.175 --> 00:12:04.125
Then, create a fill-in-the-blank

00:12:04.155 --> 00:12:05.995
exercise where you practise

00:12:06.295 --> 00:12:07.525
using those expressions.

00:12:08.205 --> 00:12:10.855
Finally, get AI to give you a

00:12:10.895 --> 00:12:13.875
discussion question about the episode

00:12:13.925 --> 00:12:15.965
topic, and practise answering it.

00:12:16.725 --> 00:12:19.605
This can turn 20 or 30 minutes

00:12:19.825 --> 00:12:22.275
of passive listening into an

00:12:22.335 --> 00:12:24.495
hour plus of active learning.

00:12:25.085 --> 00:12:27.395
And you can do this with literally any

00:12:27.415 --> 00:12:29.905
podcast or video that has a transcript.

00:12:30.545 --> 00:12:32.835
And by the way, you can find lots

00:12:32.835 --> 00:12:36.405
of demos and tutorials of how to

00:12:36.415 --> 00:12:38.725
do this on  our YouTube channel.

00:12:38.915 --> 00:12:41.845
There’s a link in the description to that.

00:12:42.525 --> 00:12:46.896
Now let's turn to output, writing and

00:12:46.896 --> 00:12:49.956
speaking, and let’s start with writing.

00:12:50.726 --> 00:12:54.146
Writing is where AI has perhaps

00:12:54.176 --> 00:12:56.926
made the biggest leap forward for

00:12:56.986 --> 00:12:59.136
English learners, and where I think

00:12:59.146 --> 00:13:01.716
the benefits are most clear-cut.

00:13:02.526 --> 00:13:04.006
But again, there are some

00:13:04.046 --> 00:13:05.476
pretty obvious pitfalls.

00:13:06.126 --> 00:13:09.786
So, the old way of improving your

00:13:09.836 --> 00:13:11.936
English writing was essentially

00:13:11.936 --> 00:13:14.016
to learn grammar and vocabulary,

00:13:14.036 --> 00:13:16.706
through textbooks and reading widely.

00:13:17.339 --> 00:13:20.146
And to turn that acquired

00:13:20.146 --> 00:13:22.176
knowledge, those building blocks

00:13:22.186 --> 00:13:24.756
of language, into written text.

00:13:25.216 --> 00:13:26.236
You’d write something.

00:13:26.686 --> 00:13:29.016
But you wouldn’t be sure if it was right.

00:13:29.556 --> 00:13:31.786
Maybe you weren’t sure you’d used the

00:13:31.786 --> 00:13:34.636
right tense, or whether this was how a

00:13:34.646 --> 00:13:36.436
native speaker would have written it.

00:13:36.966 --> 00:13:39.276
Perhaps you’d ask a friend, someone who

00:13:39.276 --> 00:13:42.166
was better at English than you, or even

00:13:42.176 --> 00:13:44.196
a native speaker if you were lucky.

00:13:44.846 --> 00:13:46.686
Maybe you were doing an English

00:13:46.726 --> 00:13:49.066
course, and you could ask your teacher.

00:13:49.716 --> 00:13:52.676
Or failing that, you might post it in a

00:13:52.686 --> 00:13:56.546
forum somewhere and wait, hoping for a

00:13:56.606 --> 00:13:59.026
kind soul to put you out of your misery.

00:13:59.706 --> 00:14:01.536
You might be lucky enough to

00:14:01.536 --> 00:14:03.576
get a response, but often the

00:14:03.576 --> 00:14:05.636
feedback wouldn’t be very helpful.

00:14:06.146 --> 00:14:08.086
Even a native speaker might

00:14:08.086 --> 00:14:10.336
not be able to tell you why

00:14:10.556 --> 00:14:12.666
something was wrong, it just was.

00:14:13.776 --> 00:14:15.956
Now, AI tools have made

00:14:15.956 --> 00:14:17.786
this significantly easier.

00:14:18.166 --> 00:14:19.196
You write something.

00:14:19.446 --> 00:14:22.736
You paste it into an AI tool, and the AI

00:14:22.836 --> 00:14:25.466
will give you a perfect, natural version.

00:14:26.206 --> 00:14:27.116
Amazing, right?

00:14:27.766 --> 00:14:29.816
But the problem with this is that

00:14:29.886 --> 00:14:32.956
I see so many non-native English

00:14:32.956 --> 00:14:36.406
speakers starting to rely on AI

00:14:36.426 --> 00:14:38.916
tools to do the writing for them.

00:14:39.806 --> 00:14:42.126
As you might imagine, I get tonnes of

00:14:42.126 --> 00:14:44.846
emails, mostly from non-native speakers.

00:14:45.166 --> 00:14:47.846
It is immediately obvious to me

00:14:48.206 --> 00:14:50.336
who has used an AI to write the

00:14:50.336 --> 00:14:52.576
email for them and who hasn’t.

00:14:53.186 --> 00:14:56.536
And I have to admit that I am infinitely

00:14:56.556 --> 00:14:59.226
more impressed with those people who

00:14:59.416 --> 00:15:01.696
haven’t, the people who have forced

00:15:01.696 --> 00:15:05.326
themselves to produce English, even

00:15:05.326 --> 00:15:07.736
when there is the tempting option of

00:15:07.736 --> 00:15:09.736
getting an AI tool to write it for you.

00:15:10.586 --> 00:15:13.176
Now, you might argue that people will

00:15:13.176 --> 00:15:16.026
just have to get used to this, and in

00:15:16.026 --> 00:15:19.346
a world where AI agents outnumber human

00:15:19.346 --> 00:15:22.306
employees, and AI-generated English

00:15:22.476 --> 00:15:25.676
continues to improve, nobody will need to

00:15:25.926 --> 00:15:28.516
actually write; an AI will do it for them.

00:15:29.476 --> 00:15:32.948
Even if that will some day be true, there

00:15:32.948 --> 00:15:35.678
is still an important reason to work on

00:15:35.678 --> 00:15:38.258
your writing, as a non-native speaker.

00:15:39.008 --> 00:15:41.318
And that’s that improving your

00:15:41.318 --> 00:15:43.798
writing skills is a brilliant way

00:15:44.148 --> 00:15:46.318
of not just improving your all-round

00:15:46.418 --> 00:15:49.208
productive skills but helping you

00:15:49.428 --> 00:15:52.208
collect and distill your thoughts.

00:15:53.528 --> 00:15:56.998
Unlike speaking, writing is an activity

00:15:57.158 --> 00:15:58.928
you can do without anyone seeing your

00:15:58.928 --> 00:16:01.368
mistakes, it’s an activity that helps

00:16:01.378 --> 00:16:04.211
build your productive muscles, and

00:16:04.411 --> 00:16:07.701
all in a safe space, because if you

00:16:07.741 --> 00:16:10.441
change what you want to say or you

00:16:10.441 --> 00:16:12.171
realise you’ve made a mistake, you

00:16:12.171 --> 00:16:14.471
can just go back and write it again.

00:16:15.311 --> 00:16:17.151
And there are some great ways

00:16:17.491 --> 00:16:19.251
in which you can use AI to

00:16:19.251 --> 00:16:20.921
help you improve your writing.

00:16:21.441 --> 00:16:25.091
Note, help you improve, not write for you.

00:16:26.101 --> 00:16:29.121
If you use a tool like ChatGPT,

00:16:29.411 --> 00:16:31.531
you can share your writing and ask

00:16:31.571 --> 00:16:33.951
not just for a perfect version, but

00:16:33.951 --> 00:16:36.231
for explanations of the mistakes.

00:16:36.991 --> 00:16:38.901
You might already use the tool that

00:16:38.911 --> 00:16:41.591
we’ve been building, FixMyEnglish.ai,

00:16:41.701 --> 00:16:45.581
which does a similar thing, but recreates

00:16:45.591 --> 00:16:47.721
the experience you might have had with

00:16:47.721 --> 00:16:50.551
a brilliant teacher, where they cross

00:16:50.551 --> 00:16:52.691
out all of your mistakes in red pen

00:16:52.981 --> 00:16:55.721
and write extensive explanations, so

00:16:55.871 --> 00:16:58.101
that you learn from every mistake.

00:16:58.781 --> 00:17:00.471
By the way, if you haven’t checked that

00:17:00.471 --> 00:17:03.128
out yet, you can go to FixMyEnglish.ai.

00:17:03.453 --> 00:17:04.741
I’ll put a link in the

00:17:04.771 --> 00:17:06.001
show description too.

00:17:07.061 --> 00:17:09.771
If you’d prefer to use ChatGPT

00:17:09.771 --> 00:17:12.331
or Gemini, or whatever AI tool

00:17:12.331 --> 00:17:14.761
you prefer, here’s how to do it.

00:17:15.451 --> 00:17:16.711
First, write it

00:17:16.711 --> 00:17:18.541
yourself - mistakes and all.

00:17:19.111 --> 00:17:22.361
Then, instead of asking AI to

00:17:22.361 --> 00:17:25.531
rewrite it perfectly, ask it to tell

00:17:25.531 --> 00:17:27.971
you HOW MANY mistakes you made and

00:17:27.971 --> 00:17:30.651
what TYPES of errors they are, but

00:17:31.171 --> 00:17:33.551
don't ask it to fix them just yet.

00:17:34.501 --> 00:17:37.411
Then, try to find and correct the

00:17:37.411 --> 00:17:39.721
mistakes based on those hints.

00:17:40.481 --> 00:17:43.511
THEN get AI to show you the perfect

00:17:43.511 --> 00:17:46.031
version and explain what you missed.

00:17:46.721 --> 00:17:49.091
This way, you are doing

00:17:49.091 --> 00:17:50.741
the learning, not the AI.

00:17:51.111 --> 00:17:52.541
You are building the

00:17:52.541 --> 00:17:54.651
skill, not outsourcing it.

00:17:55.381 --> 00:17:57.531
It takes a bit longer, of course,

00:17:57.881 --> 00:18:00.441
but that's the point - the struggle

00:18:00.721 --> 00:18:02.511
is where the learning happens.

00:18:03.431 --> 00:18:05.391
Now, final point, let’s move

00:18:05.531 --> 00:18:07.301
on to talk about speaking.

00:18:07.971 --> 00:18:11.031
We touched on this a little bit in

00:18:11.041 --> 00:18:14.471
the listening section, but let's go a

00:18:14.471 --> 00:18:17.221
bit deeper, because speaking is almost

00:18:17.241 --> 00:18:19.901
always the skill most people want

00:18:19.951 --> 00:18:22.421
to improve and most struggle with.

00:18:22.981 --> 00:18:24.531
It’s also the scariest.

00:18:25.351 --> 00:18:28.691
Speaking to a real human, a native

00:18:28.701 --> 00:18:31.241
English speaker, can be really daunting,

00:18:31.501 --> 00:18:34.141
and practising with an AI can be

00:18:34.141 --> 00:18:36.631
a very helpful stepping stone, not

00:18:36.631 --> 00:18:39.271
because AI conversation is perfect,

00:18:39.321 --> 00:18:41.971
but because the judgment is removed.

00:18:42.451 --> 00:18:45.721
So it’s hardly surprising that this,

00:18:45.751 --> 00:18:49.141
that speaking, this is the skill that

00:18:49.151 --> 00:18:51.561
so many companies are focusing on.

00:18:52.221 --> 00:18:54.601
And if you are anything like me, you

00:18:54.601 --> 00:18:57.111
are probably bombarded with adverts

00:18:57.111 --> 00:18:59.591
for AI-tutors with all sorts of

00:18:59.591 --> 00:19:02.781
promises, from boasts about how many

00:19:02.791 --> 00:19:05.071
billions of downloads they have to

00:19:05.291 --> 00:19:08.061
promises to make you fluent in 90 days.

00:19:08.641 --> 00:19:10.101
Perhaps you’ve tried a

00:19:10.101 --> 00:19:10.911
few of them yourself.

00:19:11.901 --> 00:19:14.871
The dream that these apps are selling

00:19:15.241 --> 00:19:17.901
is this: you'll be able to have a real

00:19:18.231 --> 00:19:20.841
spoken conversation with an AI, it

00:19:20.841 --> 00:19:23.231
will understand your accent, respond

00:19:23.251 --> 00:19:25.411
naturally, correct your mistakes

00:19:25.481 --> 00:19:28.531
gently, and you'll emerge more fluent.

00:19:29.381 --> 00:19:32.671
And to be fair, the dream is closer

00:19:32.671 --> 00:19:35.641
to reality than it was in 2022.

00:19:36.198 --> 00:19:38.191
All of these tools have

00:19:38.221 --> 00:19:40.031
improved significantly.

00:19:40.431 --> 00:19:42.341
The voices sound more natural.

00:19:42.581 --> 00:19:45.191
The speech recognition is much better.

00:19:45.661 --> 00:19:47.711
But here is what they won't tell

00:19:47.711 --> 00:19:49.601
you in the marketing materials,

00:19:49.851 --> 00:19:51.871
and what you’ve probably found

00:19:51.871 --> 00:19:53.661
out yourself if you’ve used them.

00:19:54.671 --> 00:19:57.271
AI conversation practice and real

00:19:57.291 --> 00:19:59.501
conversation practice are still

00:19:59.561 --> 00:20:01.891
very far from the same thing.

00:20:02.791 --> 00:20:04.121
When you talk to an AI,

00:20:04.421 --> 00:20:06.521
it is infinitely patient.

00:20:06.671 --> 00:20:08.311
It never misunderstands you

00:20:08.571 --> 00:20:10.511
in the way a real person does.

00:20:10.671 --> 00:20:12.581
It doesn't have an agenda, it's not

00:20:12.581 --> 00:20:14.971
distracted, it doesn't use slang

00:20:15.251 --> 00:20:16.601
you've never heard, it doesn't

00:20:16.601 --> 00:20:18.731
speak faster when it gets excited.

00:20:19.181 --> 00:20:20.951
It will wait, calmly,

00:20:21.421 --> 00:20:22.951
for as long as you need.

00:20:24.411 --> 00:20:26.241
That sounds like a good thing.

00:20:26.751 --> 00:20:28.521
And in many ways it is.

00:20:28.921 --> 00:20:31.771
But it also means that you're practising

00:20:32.101 --> 00:20:34.491
a version of conversation that

00:20:34.491 --> 00:20:36.991
doesn't quite exist in the real world.

00:20:38.131 --> 00:20:40.611
Now, none of this means

00:20:40.621 --> 00:20:42.081
these tools are useless.

00:20:42.421 --> 00:20:43.131
Far from it.

00:20:43.551 --> 00:20:45.581
If you genuinely use a speaking

00:20:45.581 --> 00:20:48.221
tool every day, you will get more

00:20:48.221 --> 00:20:49.741
comfortable speaking English.

00:20:50.051 --> 00:20:52.971
That has real value, especially if

00:20:52.971 --> 00:20:54.601
you don't have many opportunities

00:20:54.721 --> 00:20:56.521
to practise with real people.

00:20:57.151 --> 00:21:01.551
But go in with realistic expectations, and

00:21:01.791 --> 00:21:04.881
don't let it replace real conversation.

00:21:05.931 --> 00:21:08.181
The way I suggest thinking about

00:21:08.181 --> 00:21:10.711
it is like a rehearsal space.

00:21:11.291 --> 00:21:13.051
Actors don't rehearse

00:21:13.081 --> 00:21:14.261
in front of an audience.

00:21:14.561 --> 00:21:16.611
They rehearse in an empty room,

00:21:16.931 --> 00:21:20.151
repeatedly, until the words feel natural.

00:21:20.751 --> 00:21:22.671
Then they go on stage.

00:21:23.231 --> 00:21:26.261
AI conversation can be your empty

00:21:26.261 --> 00:21:28.431
room — the place where you practise

00:21:28.691 --> 00:21:30.631
until it stops feeling frightening.

00:21:31.381 --> 00:21:34.941
But, remember, the point of learning

00:21:34.941 --> 00:21:37.841
English is to use it in the real world,

00:21:38.071 --> 00:21:41.471
and even though an AI tutor might feel

00:21:41.561 --> 00:21:44.631
comfortable, reassuring, and familiar,

00:21:44.991 --> 00:21:48.761
its only value is in helping you prepare

00:21:48.861 --> 00:21:51.671
for the real world, real conversation.

00:21:52.241 --> 00:21:54.661
And the longer you postpone that,

00:21:55.021 --> 00:21:57.091
the more scary it will be, no

00:21:57.091 --> 00:21:59.031
matter what your “pronunciation

00:21:59.031 --> 00:22:01.141
score” is on your favourite app.

00:22:01.991 --> 00:22:04.121
And, by the way, you don’t have to

00:22:04.351 --> 00:22:06.961
use any kind of third-party app to

00:22:07.171 --> 00:22:09.951
“chat with AI”. Most of the big AI

00:22:09.961 --> 00:22:13.111
companies will have a “voice mode”, so

00:22:13.261 --> 00:22:15.651
just open that up and start talking.

00:22:15.961 --> 00:22:17.661
You don’t need to pay for anything

00:22:17.661 --> 00:22:19.341
else unless you really want to.

00:22:20.261 --> 00:22:23.731
And a quick practical tip here is to

00:22:23.731 --> 00:22:26.491
set it up properly before you go into

00:22:26.491 --> 00:22:29.301
voice mode, so type out whatever you

00:22:29.301 --> 00:22:32.471
want to practise first, and any prompt

00:22:32.501 --> 00:22:35.441
instructions, then switch to voice.

00:22:36.541 --> 00:22:38.621
Now, I know we’ve talked

00:22:38.631 --> 00:22:39.801
about quite a lot here.

00:22:40.251 --> 00:22:42.511
It can sound overwhelming, so

00:22:42.511 --> 00:22:44.951
many different potential tools,

00:22:45.161 --> 00:22:47.891
chatbots, AI-tutors, vocabulary

00:22:47.891 --> 00:22:50.071
apps, apps that promise to

00:22:50.171 --> 00:22:52.181
personalise your English learning.

00:22:52.951 --> 00:22:55.041
The truth is that there is still

00:22:55.151 --> 00:22:57.511
one thing that matters more

00:22:57.511 --> 00:22:59.841
than all of this: consistency.

00:23:00.191 --> 00:23:02.931
Actually consuming and then producing

00:23:02.941 --> 00:23:05.871
English regularly, daily if possible.

00:23:06.471 --> 00:23:09.941
Someone who listens to podcasts, BBC News,

00:23:10.201 --> 00:23:12.461
and reads an English-language newspaper

00:23:12.511 --> 00:23:15.631
every day without ever touching AI will

00:23:15.631 --> 00:23:18.701
make far faster progress than someone who

00:23:18.701 --> 00:23:21.681
spends weeks researching which AI-tutor

00:23:21.721 --> 00:23:25.141
to subscribe to, then downloads five and

00:23:25.141 --> 00:23:27.281
waits for the perfect moment to start.

00:23:28.391 --> 00:23:31.141
And let me end this by talking about

00:23:31.141 --> 00:23:33.861
my own personal experience here,

00:23:34.071 --> 00:23:36.461
as I am actively learning Swedish.

00:23:37.141 --> 00:23:39.141
As you might imagine, I have all sorts

00:23:39.141 --> 00:23:41.711
of AI companies contacting me and

00:23:41.901 --> 00:23:43.831
offering me things to promote their

00:23:43.881 --> 00:23:48.401
app, so I have loads of free accounts.

00:23:48.921 --> 00:23:52.321
And the truth is that I barely use them.

00:23:52.951 --> 00:23:56.291
I listen to Swedish podcasts, I try to

00:23:56.291 --> 00:23:59.741
read real Swedish, I go to Swedish class.

00:24:00.101 --> 00:24:03.441
I use AI to get feedback on my writing,

00:24:03.631 --> 00:24:06.941
to explain grammar points to me, and I

00:24:06.961 --> 00:24:10.931
sometimes talk to ChatGPT in Swedish.

00:24:11.411 --> 00:24:14.271
Perhaps this makes me old-fashioned,

00:24:14.541 --> 00:24:17.641
but I know firsthand that actually

00:24:17.651 --> 00:24:19.821
doing the work is far more

00:24:19.831 --> 00:24:22.451
important than how you do the work.

00:24:23.151 --> 00:24:25.751
Learning English, like any language,

00:24:25.931 --> 00:24:28.541
is about consistency, attitude,

00:24:28.911 --> 00:24:30.731
and materials that interest you.

00:24:31.481 --> 00:24:33.301
AI won’t make you fluent.

00:24:33.531 --> 00:24:35.561
But it can make consistent

00:24:35.571 --> 00:24:37.601
learners even more effective.

00:24:39.071 --> 00:24:41.531
OK, then, that is it for today's episode

00:24:41.731 --> 00:24:45.271
on AI & Learning English in 2026.

00:24:45.541 --> 00:24:46.861
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:24:47.151 --> 00:24:49.081
and that you've learnt something new.

00:24:49.701 --> 00:24:51.241
As always, I would love to know

00:24:51.241 --> 00:24:52.961
what you thought of this episode.

00:24:53.321 --> 00:24:55.801
How, if at all, do you use

00:24:55.801 --> 00:24:57.551
AI to improve your English?

00:24:57.771 --> 00:24:59.541
What are your tools of choice?

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:02.551
Plain old ChatGPT, or something else?

00:25:02.881 --> 00:25:03.971
You can head right into our

00:25:03.971 --> 00:25:05.461
community forum, which is at

00:25:05.461 --> 00:25:08.091
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:25:08.091 --> 00:25:10.351
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:25:11.011 --> 00:25:12.951
You've been listening to English Learning

00:25:12.951 --> 00:25:15.701
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:16.081 --> 00:25:18.831
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:18.831 --> 00:25:21.121
I'll catch you in the next episode.

